  <!-- ITEM 17: MYCOPLASMA COLD AGGLUTININS -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 17</span>
      <span>Category: Microbiology & Hematology</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      A 19-year-old military recruit presents with 2 weeks of dry cough, malaise, and low-grade fever. Physical examination reveals mild pallor and scattered bilateral rhonchi. A peripheral blood smear exhibits red blood cell clumping at room temperature that dissolves upon warming to 37°C. Sputum PCR confirms <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following immunoglobulins and target erythrocyte surface antigens mediates this autoimmune agglutination phenomenon?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Monomeric IgG antibodies directed against the erythrocyte Rh(D) protein antigen</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> Pentameric IgM autoantibodies directed against the erythrocyte I carbohydrate antigen</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Secretory dimeric IgA antibodies directed against glycophorin A surface glycoproteins</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Circulating IgE antibodies directed against spectrin intracellular scaffolding fibers</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Polyclonal IgG antibodies binding directly to Duffy chemokine surface receptors</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: B</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em> shares antigenic mimicry with erythrocyte membrane carbohydrates. Infection triggers the production of <strong>cold agglutinins: polyclonal IgM antibodies directed against the I antigen on human erythrocytes</strong>. At lower temperatures (e.g., in peripheral acral capillary beds at 0°C to 4°C), these pentameric IgM antibodies bind erythrocytes and fix complement, leading to agglutination, transient acrocyanosis, and mild extravascular hemolysis.
