  <!-- ITEM 20: HERBAL COUGH THERAPY (DRIED IVY EXTRACT) -->
  <div class="question-card">
    <div class="meta-bar">
      <span>Question 20</span>
      <span>Category: Pharmacology of Cough</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      The parents of a 5-year-old girl present to an outpatient clinic seeking a safe over-the-counter remedy for their daughter's dry, irritating cough associated with a mild viral upper respiratory tract infection. The mother is hesitant to give opioid suppressants or synthetic chemical antitussives and requests an evidence-based plant-derived herbal preparation.
    </div>
    <div class="stem">
      Which of the following botanical extracts is clinically indicated in pediatric patients to relieve respiratory spasms and facilitate secretolysis?
    </div>
    <ul class="options">
      <li><strong>A.</strong> Standardized dried ivy leaf extract (<em>Hedera helix</em>)</li>
      <li><strong>B.</strong> St. John's Wort extract (<em>Hypericum perforatum</em>)</li>
      <li><strong>C.</strong> Concentrated Gingko biloba leaf extract</li>
      <li><strong>D.</strong> Purified evening primrose oil suspension</li>
      <li><strong>E.</strong> Standardized black cohosh root extract</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="explanation">
      <div class="correct-badge">Correct Answer: A</div>
      <div class="rationale">
        <strong>Dried ivy leaf extract (<em>Hedera helix</em>)</strong> is a recognized herbal preparation used in cough syrups (e.g., Prospan) for children and adults. Its active triterpene saponins (notably hederacoside C and alpha-hederin) inhibit the internalization of beta2-adrenergic receptors in airway smooth muscle cells and alveolar type II cells. This produces dual therapeutic actions: <strong>secretolysis</strong> (reduces mucus viscosity by promoting surfactant release) and <strong>bronchospasmolysis</strong> (smooth muscle relaxation).
      </div>
      <div class="distractors">
        <strong>Distractor Breakdown:</strong>
